        "titel": "4.1 Societies: the opening and the big picture",
        "tekst": "**The opening story.** In **June 2008** the press got excited about the sighting of an 'unknown and unexplored' tribe in the Amazon basin. A plane over the Brazilian state of Acre (on the border with Peru) saw people around two communal huts; on its return, most women and children had fled and three figures aimed **bows and arrows at the sky**. 'We did the overflight to show their houses, to show they are there, to show they exist', said **José Carlos dos Reis Meirelles Junior**. A long history of earlier societies destroyed by 'newcomers' who plunder, bring aggression and spread disease gave every reason to fear strangers. The **Akuntsu** in Rondônia are now reduced to **five individuals** and will soon be extinct: we will lose another unique society. (Photo caption: Brazil today has around 650,000 Indians in over 200 tribes; perhaps 40 groups of truly isolated people remain.)\n\nTwo quotes: **Margaret Thatcher**: 'There is no such thing as society.' **David Cameron**: 'We are taking to help make the Big Society a reality.' The chapter asks: what are the main types of society in history? How can we explain the rise of modern industrial societies? What are the broad shapes of contemporary world societies?\n\n**The big picture.** In **2010** there were around **200 major societies** (192 in the UN), but counting small groups, perhaps **7,000 languages**, usually indicating different social groupings. Sociology was born out of concern with the **decline of traditional societies** and the rapidly changing modern industrial world. The central concept: **[[society|Society]]**: *people who interact in a defined space and share culture*. Both Europe and countries such as Norway and England can be seen as societies.\n\nThe chapter then previews three founders: **Marx** (history as economic conflict: the economy generates **conflicts and inequalities** around production, the motor of change), **Weber** (the power of **human ideas**, especially in religion; rational thinking underlies modern society) and **Durkheim** (patterns of **social solidarity**: the bonds of traditional and modern societies differ). *(Their full theories come later in the chapter, beyond the assigned pages.)*"
