        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "4.6 Industrial societies",
        "tekst": "**[[Industrialism]]**: *technology that powers sophisticated machinery with advanced sources of energy*. Before it, the main energy was human and animal muscle; from the **Industrial Revolution (about 1750)** mills and factories used water and later steam. Societies changed faster (**Figure 4.1**: the number of innovations in western Europe exploded from the eighteenth century, because each innovation spins off further ones): more change in a century than in thousands of years before, which stimulated the birth of sociology. Railways, steamships and skyscrapers; the combustion engine and electricity; telephone, radio and television making the world smaller; flight, nuclear power, and in the last generation computers and the **Information Revolution**.\n\n- **Work** moved from home and land to **factories**; close working relationships, strong kinship ties and many traditional values were lost.\n- **Unparalleled prosperity**: after poor health in early industrial cities, living standards and medicine raised life expectancy and population. Industrialisation draws people to cities: **one in ten** people in agrarian societies lives in cities, **three in four** in industrial ones.\n- **Occupational specialisation** grows: people size each other up by **jobs** rather than kinship; rapid change and mobility bring **anonymity**, cultural diversity, subcultures and countercultures.\n- The **family** loses its traditional significance as the centre of production, learning and worship; more single people, divorced people, single parents, lesbian and gay couples and stepfamilies.\n- Early industrialisation concentrated benefits on a few; later they spread: the standard of living has risen **fivefold** in a century and inequality has declined, because industrial societies need a **literate, skilled labour force** (state schooling, political rights for nearly everyone). Industrialisation intensifies **demands for political participation** (South Korea, Taiwan, China, the former Soviet Union, eastern Europe)."
