        "tekst": "In the early 1970s **Daniel Bell** (1976) coined **[[post-industrialism|Post-industrialism]]**: *computer-linked technology that supports an information-based economy*. Production shifts from factories and material goods to computers and devices that create, process, store and apply **information**: the information society, the network society, the cyber-society, the **postmodern society**. Skills shift from mechanical to **information-based**; fewer people work in industry, more as clerical workers, managers and information processors, with more mental skills for fragmented, multi-tasking work. The worldwide flow of information ties societies together and extends **globalisation** (chapters 2, 6 and 23). **Postmodernism**: *ways of thinking which stress a plurality of perspectives as opposed to a unified, single core*: change speeds up, classical boundaries break down.\n\nAll **five kinds of society still coexist**: some societies focus on agriculture and the industrial world still dominates, but change keeps accelerating. **Table 4.1** summarises the five types."
