        "titel": "4.8 Technological determinism: a cautious word",
        "tekst": "Technology may create preconditions for different societies, but four cautions (three are on the assigned pages):\n1. **Technology does not determine society.** People decide how to use it, in very different ways: Nazi Germany used modern technology to exterminate millions; the Incas and Egyptians had sophisticated technology and also domination and slavery. **Technology is neutral: it is people who shape technology.**\n2. **No automatic evolution.** The five types do not simply evolve into one another; in the twenty-first century they **coexist**, and many indigenous peoples have highly evolved technologies of their own. Seeing them as prior to or simpler than European culture is a **Eurocentric** view.\n3. **The limits of technology.** Technology raises productivity, eliminates disease and relieves boredom, but is **no 'quick fix'** for deep social problems: poverty remains the plight of billions, and technology creates new problems our ancestors could not imagine. Industrial societies give more personal freedom, often at a cost (the reading stops here; the fourth caution is on the next page)."
